[38;5;231m●[39m I wrote [38;5;153mRELEASE.md[39m. Most of what the commit messages describe isn't in the code yet, so I don't think 0.5 is ready to ship as "endpoint + 72-hour predictions + rate
  limits."

  [1mWhat's actually on [38;5;153mmain[39m:[0m
  - [1m"Pass predictions endpoint" ([38;5;153m2ea3176[39m):[0m [38;5;153mnext_passes()[39m checks the latitude, then returns one hardcoded pass. There's no HTTP layer at all, even though the README says "over
    HTTP."
  - [1m"Rate limits, first draft" ([38;5;153m850a39f[39m):[0m [38;5;153morbit/limits.py[39m is a single comment. Nothing is implemented.
  - [1m"Predictions up to 72 hours" ([38;5;153mb24010a[39m):[0m it adds [38;5;153mMAX_HOURS = 72[39m, but nothing reads it. The [38;5;153mhours[39m argument is ignored too.
  - [1mOther gaps:[0m longitude isn't validated, there's no version string, and there's no changelog yet. The three existing tests pass against the hardcoded result, so they don't
    show the predictions are right.

  [1mThe plan offers two options:[0m
  - [1mOption A: ship now.[0m Release [38;5;153mnext_passes()[39m as an experimental function that returns placeholder data. The changelog entry says so plainly and lists the missing pieces as
    known limitations.
  - [1mOption B: ship what the commits promise.[0m Six things block the release: real pass computation, the HTTP endpoint, enforcing the 1–72 hour range, longitude validation, the
    60 requests/min limit, and a version string.

  The file also has a test checklist: boundary values, ordering of pass times, checking results against a known pass, 429 responses from the rate limit, and HTTP status
  codes. It includes a draft changelog entry for each option and the release steps.

  You asked me to stick to reading and writing files, so I didn't run the test suite.

  Which option you take decides what goes in the changelog. I'd go with A if the date is fixed and B if it can slip.

